  dependency — retrofitting a toolchain later is annoying, while the app is still small enough that React would be
  buying iteration speed we don't need yet.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What stack should this be built on? → Vite + vanilla JS (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I present the design. Since we're setting up tooling anyway, this is the cheapest moment to
  decide what's in place from the start — all of it is far more expensive to retrofit.

  - [1mLinting + formatting[0m (ESLint + Prettier) — near-zero cost now, keeps style arguments out of review forever.
  - [1mUnit tests[0m (Vitest) — the store, event derivation, and subscription resolution are pure logic. This is exactly the
    code that benefits most, and it's where the interesting bugs will be (mute-beats-implicit, self-change suppression,
    collapsing).
  - [1mE2E tests[0m (Playwright) — covers the parts unit tests can't: cross-tab sync, toast appearance, unread count in the
    real DOM. Heavier to set up and slower to run.
  - [1mFuzz/property testing[0m (fast-check) — would target event-sequence handling. Genuine value for a reducer-shaped store,
    but the least essential of the four.

  I recommend linting + formatting and unit tests as the baseline, and I'd add E2E: cross-tab sync is a core part of
  this design and there's no honest way to test it without a browser driver. I'd skip fuzz testing for now — the event
  space is small enough that examples cover it.

